name: the editor
boss: Mux
flavor: Detach all you like. I keep the session.
brief: vi is on every unix that has ever existed and it is on this one. You do
brief: not have to like it; you have to be able to leave it, and to fix a
brief: config file on a machine that has nothing else.
brief:
brief: Two modes. You start in normal mode, where keys are commands. i enters
brief: insert mode, where keys are text. Escape returns. Everything else is
brief: detail you can pick up later.
brief:
brief: The one you must know: press Escape, then type :q! and Enter. That
brief: leaves without saving, from any state you have got yourself into.
---
q: {open @txt@ in the editor|open @txt@ in vi|start editing @txt@ in vi}
a: vi @txt@
ref: vi
teach: vi
hint: vi with just a filename opens it for editing, ready in normal mode
---
q: {open @txt@ read-only, so you cannot damage it by accident|open @txt@ in vi read-only, so a stray edit cannot land|view @txt@ in vi without being able to save changes}
a: vi -R @txt@
ref: vi
teach: vi -R
hint: -R opens the file but blocks writes until you override it
---
q: {leave vi without saving, from normal mode|quit vi from normal mode, discarding any changes|exit vi without saving, from normal mode}
a: :q!
ref: vi
teach: vi
hint: escape first, then colon
---
q: {save and leave vi|write the file and quit vi|save your changes and exit vi}
a: :wq
a: :x
ref: vi
teach: vi
hint: :wq and :x both save and quit; :x only writes if something changed
---
q: {save without leaving|save the file without quitting vi|write the file but stay in vi}
a: :w
ref: vi
teach: vi
hint: :w writes the file without leaving normal mode
---
q: {jump to line 20 inside vi|move the cursor to line 20 in vi|go straight to line 20 in vi}
a: :20
ref: vi
teach: vi
hint: a bare :N in command mode jumps straight to line N
---
q: {search forward for @term@ inside vi|search forward in vi for @term@|from vi, search forward for @term@}
a: /@term@
ref: vi
teach: vi
hint: / starts a forward search, just like less and man
---
q: {replace every occurrence of @old@ with @new@ in the whole file, in vi|in vi, replace every occurrence of @old@ with @new@ across the whole file|from vi, swap every @old@ for @new@ everywhere in the file}
a: :%s/@old@/@new@/g
ref: vi
teach: vi
hint: the same s/// you already know from sed
---
q: {delete the current line in vi|remove the current line in vi|delete the line the cursor is on, in vi}
a: dd
ref: vi
teach: vi
hint: dd in vi deletes (and yanks) the whole current line
---
q: {undo the last change in vi|undo your last edit in vi|step back one change in vi}
a: u
ref: vi
teach: vi
hint: u undoes one change at a time, like most editors
---
q: {page through @log@ with the pager rather than the editor|view @log@ a page at a time using less, not vi|open @log@ in less instead of the editor}
a: less @log@
ref: less
teach: less
hint: less pages through a file without letting you edit it
---
q: {inside less, search for @term@|from within less, search for @term@|while paging with less, search for @term@}
a: /@term@
ref: less
teach: less
hint: less shares vi's / search binding
---
q: {inside less, jump to the end of the file|from within less, jump straight to the end of the file|while in less, go to the end of the file}
a: G
ref: less
teach: less
hint: G jumps to the very end, the same key vi uses for the last line
---
q: {follow a growing file from inside less, the way tail -f does|from within less, follow a growing file the way tail -f does|switch less into tail -f style follow mode}
a: F
ref: less
teach: less
hint: F switches less into a live-follow mode, same idea as tail -f
---
setup: The shell itself has a vi mode. Turn it on and the command line you type
setup: at the prompt uses the same keys you just learned in the editor: Escape
setup: for normal mode, then h j k l, w, dd, and / to search your history.
q: %turn% on vi keybindings at the shell prompt
a: set -o vi
ref: set
teach: set -o
hint: set -o vi swaps the shell's line editor to vi keys
